    "question": {
      ":question/id": "025fb705-7af9-5d10-a75f-bcfd839d8c3c",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following diagrams gives the slope field at the points indicated for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2x+(3)y}{3}?$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We have to sketch the slope field at the points $(2,3)$,$(-1,2)$,$(-2,3)$.\n\nTo sketch the slope field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the right-hand side of the differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(2,3)}=\\frac{2(2)+(3)(3)}{3}=\\frac{13}{3}, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-1,2)}=\\frac{2(-1)+(3)(2)}{3}=\\frac{4}{3}, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-2,3)}=\\frac{2(-2)+(3)(3)}{3}=\\frac{5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting up our points and drawing our line segments, we get the following diagram.\n\n![](17592186172038/runtime-0/17592186172038/plots/8e2cff8afa91a3f4e0c384e57fc17d9a89ad4cd85887fd6cad849caedcfbf582.png)",
