    "question": {
      ":question/id": "0e1c7e4b-b1b8-5fbb-9b48-db64ceaaf205",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following diagrams gives the slope field at the points indicated for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(-2)x^{2}-(3)y?$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We have to sketch the slope field at the points $(-2,0)$,$(-1,-1)$,$(-1,3)$.\n\nTo sketch the slope field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the right-hand side of the differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-2,0)}=(-2)(-2)^{2}-3(0)=-8, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-1,-1)}=(-2)(-1)^{2}-3(-1)=1, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-1,3)}=(-2)(-1)^{2}-3(3)=-11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting up our points and drawing our line segments, we get the following diagram.\n\n![](17592186172038/runtime-0/17592186172038/plots/8703e8a9cab8129e5e0c74ae78a1e5e2ddf35c3a9c13a398ed08017906c60de7.png)",
