  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186172038,
      "id": "b0e7e0a0-9388-5346-ab96-f2dec237c9f5",
      "title": "Sketching the Slope Field of a Differential Equation at Some Isolated Points"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "f7c5a256-ae04-5d3c-8c17-ca0c68773643",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following diagrams gives the slope field at the points indicated for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=1x+(1)y?$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We have to sketch the slope field at the points $(3,1)$,$(-1,3)$,$(-2,1)$.\n\nTo sketch the slope field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the right-hand side of the differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(3,1)}=1(3)+(1)(1)=4, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-1,3)}=1(-1)+(1)(3)=2, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-2,1)}=1(-2)+(1)(1)=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting up our points and drawing our line segments, we get the following diagram.\n\n![](17592186172038/runtime-0/17592186172038/plots/59dd834cb5664b08e1eb4344692765033f15ccbe280b10a8cf5973facba54752.png)",
